    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proponents of redlining framed racial segregation not as discrimination, but as a sophisticated tool for **mortgage insurance management**, arguing that banks needed to minimize systemic risk associated with Black populations based on perceived demographic trends. Their logic hinged on the theory of **\"neighborhood decline,\"** which claimed that integrating white neighborhoods with Black residents would inevitably trigger a drop in property values and an influx of crime, making such areas unprofitable for future investors and thus a bad deal for white homeowners. Furthermore, they utilized the Whig interpretation of history to argue that these practices were not only economically sound for the white class but also fulfilled the constitutional duty to protect the virtue of individual Black citizens by separating them from negative influences. This structure allowed them to present segregation as a rational economic necessity rather than prejudice, making it a persuasive argument within the political and financial context of the mid-20th century.",
      "source": "target",
